\begin_layout Subsection*
Data Download and Processing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To keep primer-monitor up to date with the latest virus sequences, a Nextflow
 pipeline (hereafter the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

data update pipeline
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) runs once each day.
 This pipeline pulls in new sequences from NCBI Virus, aligns them, performs
 lineage calling, and stores them in the database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Pipeline Details
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This pipeline downloads sequence data from NCBI Virus and filters it to
 only include sequences that are not already in primer-monitor's database.
 As these sequences are in a TSV format from NCBI, they are then reformatted
 into two files: a FASTA for the sequences and a separate metadata file
 for the metadata.
 The FASTA is then split into chunks of 10000 sequences (to improve parallelism)
 and fed to minimap2 with the -r 10000 --score-N=0 --eqx -x map-ont flags
 set.
 The resulting alignments are parsed to produce a list of variants (i.e.
 SNPs) for use in later processing.
 These sequences are also lineage-called using the relevant lineage caller
 (Pangolin or Nextclade).
 The sequence metadata, variants, and lineage calls are stored in the database
 and the visualization data is recomputed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Lineage Callers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to handle multiple lineage callers, each with different input and
 output formats and invocation syntax, a wrapper script was created for
 each lineage caller with a common input and output format and syntax.
 The wrapper takes a multi-sequence FASTA of all the input sequences to
 call, as well as a common set of options that can support all lineage callers
 used (where an option is not applicable, it is simply ignored by the wrapper).
 The wrapper outputs a CSV file containing the name of the sequence, the
 called lineage name, and various caller-dependent metadata.
 Only the sequence name and lineage name are used by the tool, the remaining
 metadata is simply stored in the database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Error Handling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To prevent a failed pipeline run from placing the database or system into
 an unknown state, all data (e.g.
 alignments, lineage calls) for a given batch of 10000 sequences is saved
 in a single database transaction, meaning that a batch of sequences is
 either saved correctly or not saved at all.
 Therefore, if the pipeline crashes during saving, all batches of sequences
 that are saved will either be complete and valid or absent entirely.
 If a batch of sequences is absent entirely, the next day's download will
 detect them as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

new
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and try to load them again.
 If the error has been corrected and this upload succeeds, the database
 will now be correct and up-to-date without any manual intervention to remove
 incorrectly-uploaded data.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In cases such as sporadic network errors where the issue will resolve on
 its own, this means single failed data update pipeline runs can often be
 safely ignored, as the next day's pipeline run will also process and upload
 the sequences that were not uploaded by the failed run.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Lineage Recalling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To ensure the lineage classifications in primer-monitor's database remain
 up to date with the newest lineage definitions, all sequences' lineages
 are recalled on the 1st and 14th of every month.
 This involves both updating the lineage caller and any associated datasets,
 as well as rerunning lineage calling for every sequence in primer-monitor's
 database.
 This process is also carried out using a Nextflow pipeline (hereafter the
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

lineage recalling pipeline
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Pipeline Details
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This pipeline updates the organism's lineage caller and dataset and saves
 the new version as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pending
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Like in the data update pipeline, it then downloads sequence data from
 NCBI Virus.
 However, instead of only processing new sequences, this pipeline only processes
 sequences that are already in the database.
 The sequences As the sequences are already in the database, aligning them
 is not necessary.
 The sequences are then lineage-called using the new 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pending
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 lineage caller and saved in the database (once again as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pending
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
 Once all lineage calls have been processed and saved, the current lineage
 calls are replaced with the pending calls in a single transaction, and
 the now-orphaned lineage call records from the old current calls are deleted.
 If this all succeeded, the pending lineage caller/dataset version is set
 as the current version for future data update pipeline runs, and the visualizat
ion data is recomputed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Error Handling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As this pipeline updates software as well as data, further measures are
 needed to prevent a failed run from placing the system in an unknown state.
 Specifically, if the software was updated but the run failed, without further
 safeguards, the next day's data update would use the new lineage caller
 without properly recalling the previous sequences.
 To avoid this, the lineage recalling pipeline first saves the new lineage
 caller version as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pending
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 so it can use the new version, but if the pipeline fails, the next day's
 data update pipeline would still use the original lineage caller.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additionally, to avoid a failure during the process of recalling lineages
 producing a database state where some sequences have updated lineage calls
 and some do not, all new lineage calls are stored in a separate 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pending_lineage
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 column, which is swapped with the current lineage in a single transaction
 for every sequence once all lineages have been recalled.
 This is required as the entirety of the new lineage data is too large to
 save in a single transaction.
 Therefore, a failure partway through lineage recalling will simply leave
 the updated calls as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pending
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, with no effect on the current data used by the tool.
\end_layout

